    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In his illustrations for Dante’s Divine Comedy, William Blake’s depictions of the Pilgrim communicate Blake’s unique interpretation of atonement theology. Blake uses his Divine Comedy images to “correct” the traditional Christian interpretation of the crucifixion as a sacrificial atonement for Original Sin. The depictions of Christ and crucifixion imagery in his Dante illustrations echo motifs Blake established in earlier works, such as “Jerusalem” and Paradise Lost. Blake portrays Christ’s death as a willing self-annihilation that eradicates what he calls the “Selfhood.” Through this act, Blake asserts, Christ embodies the Human Form Divine, the state in which the imaginative senses and physical reality are perfectly integrated. Blake includes motifs of both upside-down crosses and human figures in inverted cruciform poses to signal “errors” in Christian thinking that he believed impede humanity from becoming the Human Form Divine. Blake also draws on the late-Medieval technique of using proleptic iconography–which links the Christ child with imagery invoking his future crucifixion and resurrection–to depict the Pilgrim’s own eventual Christ-like spiritual transcendence in Paradiso.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
